<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">6090575</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2187464</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">85008614</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Articles</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Carboxylic ionophores enhance the cytotoxic potency of ligand- and antibody-delivered ricin A chain</article-title></title-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>10</month><year>1984</year></pub-date><volume>160</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>1234</fpage><lpage>1240</lpage><permissions></permissions><abstract><p>The highly selective cytotoxicity of site-directed ricin A chain conjugates can be potentiated by membrane-active carboxylic ionophores. The combined use of the two agents results in much faster inactivation of ribosomes and subsequent cell death and lysis. The potency of A chain cytotoxins is correspondingly increased by several orders of magnitude and cells that sparsely express the target antigen or receptor can be killed.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>